  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>If we add ferric chloride over potassium ferrocyanide, it forms a white precipitate that will become blue. The reaction is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#10230;</mo><mi>KFe</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>CN</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>KCl</mi></math></p><p>Being small quantity of ferric chloride, not all potassium is displaced.</p>"
